# Prefer Mermaid Diagrams

## Rule

All diagrams, flowcharts, and visual representations in documentation shall use Mermaid notation instead of ASCII art or other text-based diagram formats.

## Rationale

- **Consistency**: Mermaid provides a standardised syntax across all diagrams
- **Maintainability**: Easier to update and modify than ASCII art
- **Rendering**: GitHub, GitLab, Notion, and many tools render Mermaid natively
- **Accessibility**: Structured format is more accessible than visual ASCII approximations
- **Tooling**: Can be exported to SVG/PNG via Mermaid Live or similar tools

## Applies To

- Architecture diagrams
- Flowcharts and workflows
- Sequence diagrams
- Entity-relationship diagrams
- State diagrams
- User flows and wireframe layouts
- Component hierarchies

## Examples

### Flowchart

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Start] --> B{Decision}
    B -->|Yes| C[Action 1]
    B -->|No| D[Action 2]
    C --> E[End]
    D --> E
```

### Sequence Diagram

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User
    participant A as API
    participant D as Database
    U->>A: Request
    A->>D: Query
    D-->>A: Result
    A-->>U: Response
```

### State Diagram

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Draft
    Draft --> Review
    Review --> Approved
    Review --> Draft: Changes Required
    Approved --> [*]
```

### Entity Relationship

```mermaid
erDiagram
    USER ||--o{ ORDER : places
    ORDER ||--|{ LINE_ITEM : contains
    PRODUCT ||--o{ LINE_ITEM : "ordered in"
```

## Exceptions

- Inline code examples where ASCII is part of the code itself
- Terminal output representations
- Simple one-line representations (e.g., `A → B → C`)

## Migration

When updating existing documentation with ASCII diagrams:
1. Identify the diagram type (flowchart, sequence, etc.)
2. Convert to equivalent Mermaid syntax
3. Verify rendering in target platform
4. Remove ASCII version
